 ```sh
 kiln memory export                 # JSON, including correction history
+kiln memory import backup.json     # import an export into this project
 kiln memory forget test-runner     # deletes every version of this key
 kiln memory restore 12             # restores this project's version as a new record
 ```
 
+`import` reads exactly one JSON array in the `export` format from a file and
+merges it into the current project scope (or the global preferences scope with
+`kiln memory global import`), without starting a model. It never writes to the
+scopes recorded in the file: each export carries history, so it validates every
+record and imports the largest input id for each key as that key's current
+version. Project source bindings are rebound into the current project's root
+and re-verified against the destination files; records whose sources are
+missing, changed, oversized, or escape the project are rejected. Global import
+accepts preference records without sources only.
+
+```sh
+kiln memory import backup.json --dry-run
+kiln memory import backup.json --on-conflict skip
+kiln memory global import prefs.json --on-conflict replace
+```
+
+A conflict is any existing active key in the destination scope. `--on-conflict`
+chooses what to do: `error` (the default) rejects the whole batch, `skip`
+leaves existing records untouched, and `replace` supersedes them while keeping
+local correction history. `--dry-run` runs the same validation and conflict
+analysis but writes nothing. The whole selected batch is applied in one
+transaction; any validation, cancellation, or write failure leaves existing
+memories, search entries, and source bindings unchanged. On success the command
+prints one JSON object with integer `inserted`, `replaced`, and `skipped`
+counts and a boolean `dry_run`. Imports are bounded to 4 MiB and 1000 records.
+
